// Payment guardrails, v2: v1's rules plus a cap on the rolling hour.
//
// The three v1 rules are unchanged. The rolling cap is a fourth rule rather
// than a clause folded into the permit, so a refusal still names the limit it
// broke; folding would make it an implicit deny naming no rule.

// An order the entity store has never heard of is in no customer, so it matches
// no permit and is denied. A bare `resource` would authorise payments against
// order ids that do not exist, and the order id is what the agent controls.
@id("agent-may-pay")
permit (
    principal == Support::Agent::"support-bot",
    action == Support::Action::"Pay",
    resource in Support::Customer::"cus-100"
);

// Zero moves nothing and a negative amount is a charge against the customer.
@id("amount-must-be-positive")
forbid (
    principal,
    action == Support::Action::"Pay",
    resource
)
unless {
    context.input.amount_cents > 0
};

// Finance's cap, inclusive, so $100.00 exactly is allowed. A literal read from
// nothing, so no argument an agent sends raises its own ceiling.
@id("per-payment-cap")
forbid (
    principal,
    action == Support::Action::"Pay",
    resource
)
unless {
    context.input.amount_cents <= 10000
};

// The same cap again, over everything paid on this order in the last hour.
//
// The sum counts the payment being decided: it is the trace's last event, and a
// window is closed at both ends. That is forced rather than chosen. The
// temporal sublanguage has comparisons and no arithmetic (`total +
// context.input.amount_cents` is a parse error), so "the earlier payments plus
// this one" cannot be written and "the total afterwards" can.
//
// Two things below are load-bearing and fail in silence. Rows are deduplicated
// after projection onto the `for` domain, so without `(t: Timepoint)` two equal
// amounts collapse into one row and the total reads low. And `callerResource`
// is what scopes the sum to one order: drop it here and the predicate matches
// every order's payments, pooling budgets that should be separate.
//
// Nothing here can tell an executed payment from an attempted one, because a
// trace event carries no verdict. Logging only what executed is the harness's
// job, and this rule is worth nothing without it.
@id("rolling-hour-cap")
forbid (
    principal,
    action == Support::Action::"Pay",
    resource
)
unless temporal {
    exists (total: Long). (
        (sum a for (a: Long), (t: Timepoint). where (
            formerly within 1h (
                Support::Action::"Pay"::request{
                    input.amount_cents: a,
                    callerResource: resource
                }
                && tp(t)
            )
        )) == total
        && total <= 10000
    )
};
